\par\par \b \uc2 EXCLUSIVE-Vietnam may widen dong trading band again to conserve  \highlight22\uc2 FX \highlight\uc2  reserves - source\b0\par\par\uc2 697 \uc2 W\u246\'00\'F6rter\par \uc2 26 Oktober 2022\par 10:11\par \uc2 Reuters News\par \uc2 LBA\par \uc2 Englisch\par \uc2 Copyright 2022 Thomson Reuters. All Rights Reserved. \par \par \uc2 HANOI, Oct 26 (Reuters) - Vietnam is preparing to loosen its tight leash on the dong \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2 , including possibly widening its trading band with the U.S. dollar again, in order to conserve its shrinking \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  reserves, a source with direct knowledge of the matter said on Wednesday.\par \par \uc2 The dong is a managed peg to the dollar and has fallen 8% this year, pressured by capital outflows as the \uc2 U.S. Federal Reserve\uc2  repeatedly raises rates to tame inflation.\par \par \uc2 The \uc2 State Bank of \b \highlight22\uc2 Vietnam\b0 \highlight\uc2  (SBV) \uc2 has \b \highlight22\uc2 intervened\b0 \highlight\uc2  to defend the \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2 , although it rarely discloses \b \highlight22\uc2 FX\b0 \highlight\uc2  reserves data or the amount of dollars spent. Some market analysts estimate about $20 billion has been sold so far this year.\par \par \uc2 When the \uc2 SBV\uc2  last widened the band around its daily dong reference rate on Oct. 17 to 5% from 3% -- which some traders likened to a de facto devaluation -- the \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  fell to the weak end in a sign of the heavy demand for dollars.\par \par \uc2 By 0324 GMT on Wednesday, the dong was down 0.04% to 24,855 per dollar. But on the black market, it was trading at a record low of 25,370 per dollar.\par \par \uc2 The source, who refused to be identified, said the country is willing to tolerate a further 1-1.5% dong devaluation against the dollar by year end.\par \par \uc2 Widening the band, which would allow the market to drive the \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  lower without triggering so much \b \highlight22\uc2 FX\b0 \highlight\uc2  reserves selling, was one possibility, the source said.\par \par \uc2 Other measures to cushion the dong, without exhausting \b \highlight22\uc2 FX\b0 \highlight\uc2  reserves, were also being discussed, he said, without providing further details.\par \par \uc2 The timing of such moves was still under consideration, he added, but would likely be after next week's \uc2 Federal Reserve\uc2  meeting where it is expect to deliver another hefty rate hike.\par \par \uc2 "The key measure for the \uc2 SBV\uc2  to stabilise the dong is still selling dollars and buying dong, but the \uc2 SBV\uc2  is not in a good position to do that now," the source said.\par \par \uc2 SBV\uc2  had no comment on the possible widening of the trading band, saying it would be in touch with relevant agencies.\par \par \uc2 Up until September, the dong was the most stable \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  in the region, with the economy recording another quarter of strong growth and solid exports providing policymakers with plenty of hard \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  to support it.\par \par \uc2 But October alone saw a 4% erosion in the dong's value against the dollar, underperforming its peers in Asia.\par \par \uc2 Unlike its neighbours and global emerging market peers, \uc2 Vietnam's \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  only began to raise interest rates in recent weeks to combat inflation. It has now raised its policy rates twice in four weeks.\par \par \uc2 "Vietnam's response to the global situation has not been so prompt. That's why the dong is under so much pressure," the source said.\par \par \uc2 The source said the \uc2 SBV\uc2  was trying to conserve its dollars as the country's \b \highlight22\uc2 FX\b0 \highlight\uc2  reserves had sunk to the \uc2 International Monetary Fund\uc2 's recommended level, which is at least three months of imports.\par \par \uc2 Vietnam's foreign reserves have fallen significantly since the start of the year, from the peak of $112.2 billion in January to $94.5 billion in August, according to a \uc2 Fitch Solutions\uc2  report citing \uc2 IMF\uc2  data.\par \par \uc2 Around $2 billion of foreign investment inflows in the form of existing loan contracts are expected next month, which could reduce the pressure on the \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2 .\par \par \uc2 "We believe that further policy tightening will be required to stabilise the situation," said \uc2 Bank of America Securities\uc2 ' ASEAN economist Mohamed Faiz Nagutha in a note to clients on Tuesday.\par \par \uc2 "This will likely take the form of another de-facto devaluation of the dong... and/or further rate hikes," he said.\par \par \uc2 "We maintain our medium-term positive view on Vietnam, but policymakers need to draw important lessons from the current episode to modernise the monetary policy regime and make way for a more flexible exchange rate to act as a shock absorber." (Reporting by Phuong Nguyen, additional reporting by Tom Westbrook in Singapore Editing by Vidya Ranganathan and Kim Coghill)\par \par \uc2 Released: 2022-10-29T08:11:59.000Z \par \par \uc2 Dokument LBA0000020221026eiaq01kel\par }\page {\par\fs20{\*\shppict{\pict\pngblip\picw172\pich40\picscaley100\picscalex100\picwgoal2580\pichgoal600
