\par\par \b \uc2 U.S. Labels Switzerland,  \highlight22\uc2 Vietnam \highlight\uc2  Trade Cheats -- WSJ\b0\par\par\uc2 881 \uc2 W\u246\'00\'F6rter\par \uc2 17 Dezember 2020\par 08:32\par \uc2 Dow Jones Institutional News\par \uc2 DJDN\par A8\par \uc2 Englisch\par \uc2 Copyright \u169\'00\'A9 2020, Dow Jones & Company, Inc. \par \par \uc2 Treasury accuses the two of unfairly \b \highlight22\uc2 intervening\b0 \highlight\uc2  in \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  markets \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par By Kate Davidson \f28 \par \par \uc2 This article is being republished as part of our daily reproduction of WSJ.com articles that also appeared in the U.S. print edition of The Wall Street Journal (December 17, 2020).\par \par \uc2 WASHINGTON -- The \uc2 U.S. Treasury Department\uc2  labeled Switzerland and Vietnam as \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  manipulators on Wednesday, saying the two countries had \b \highlight22\uc2 intervened\b0 \highlight\uc2  in \b \highlight22\uc2 foreign-exchange\b0 \highlight\uc2  markets in a persistent, one-sided manner to limit appreciations of their \b \highlight22\uc2 currencies\b0 \highlight\uc2 .\par \par \uc2 "The Treasury Department has taken a strong step today to safeguard economic growth and opportunity for American workers and businesses," Treasury Secretary \uc2 Steven Mnuchin\uc2  said in a statement. "Treasury will follow up on its findings with respect to Vietnam and Switzerland to work toward eliminating practices that create unfair advantages for foreign competitors."\par \par \uc2 Mr. Mnuchin's statement accompanied the Treasury's \b \highlight22\uc2 foreign-exchange\b0 \highlight\uc2  report, which addresses the \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  practices and macroeconomic policies of major U.S. trading partners. The report, typically released twice a year, is the Treasury's primary vehicle for officially designating countries as \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  manipulators.\par \par \uc2 The Treasury said it determined that at least part of both countries' \b \highlight22\uc2 foreign-exchange intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2  over the past four years had aimed to prevent effective balance of payment adjustments. The agency said Vietnam had also sought to gain unfair competitive advantage in international trade through its exchange-rate management.\par \par \uc2 The Treasury Department uses three criteria to assess whether a country manipulates its exchange rate: the extent of active \b \highlight22\uc2 intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2  in \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  markets; the size of trade surpluses with the U.S.; and the size of the country's current-account surplus, a broader measure of trade that includes investment income and other financial flows.\par \par \uc2 Both Vietnam and Switzerland tripped all three criteria, a senior Treasury official said on a call with reporters Wednesday. The official said the Treasury had contacted officials in each country to notify them of the coming designation.\par \par \uc2 The action by the Treasury is mostly symbolic, requiring the U.S. administration to consult with the \uc2 International Monetary Fund\uc2  to try to eliminate the unfair advantage the \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  measures have given a country. By law, the designations trigger negotiations that could eventually lead to the imposition of tariffs, but there is no automatic penalty.\par \par \uc2 The agency also added India, Taiwan and Thailand to a formal monitoring list reserved for countries that "merit close attention to their \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  practices and macroeconomic policies." China, Japan, South Korea, Germany, Italy, Singapore and Malaysia remained on the watch list, having been placed there in previous reports.\par \par \uc2 In its last report in January, the Treasury added Switzerland to the formal monitoring list.\par \par \uc2 Switzerland has a large and rising trade surplus with the U.S. and has \b \highlight22\uc2 intervened\b0 \highlight\uc2  repeatedly in its \b \highlight22\uc2 foreign-exchange\b0 \highlight\uc2  markets in recent years. The \uc2 Swiss National Bank\uc2  has said its \b \highlight22\uc2 foreign-exchange\b0 \highlight\uc2  market \b \highlight22\uc2 interventions\b0 \highlight\uc2  are motivated by monetary-policy considerations.\par \par \uc2 The \uc2 Swiss National Bank\uc2  said on Wednesday it "remains willing to \b \highlight22\uc2 intervene\b0 \highlight\uc2  more strongly in the \b \highlight22\uc2 foreign exchange\b0 \highlight\uc2  market," citing the country's economic situation and a Swiss franc that is "still highly valued."\par \par \uc2 "Switzerland does not engage in any form of \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  manipulation," it said. \b \highlight22\uc2 Foreign exchange\b0 \highlight\uc2  market \b \highlight22\uc2 interventions\b0 \highlight\uc2  are necessary, it said, to "ensure appropriate monetary conditions and therefore price stability."\par \par \uc2 Senior Treasury officials acknowledged there may be other reasons behind Switzerland's exchange-rate management, but noted that the country's current account surplus was still extremely large.\par \par \uc2 Vietnamese Embassy officials in Washington couldn't be reached for comment.\par \par \uc2 The Treasury said in August it had determined that Vietnam depressed its \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  in 2019, the first case the department weighed in on since new regulations took effect in April allowing \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2 -focused tariffs. The administration in October launched a separate investigation into the trade practices of Vietnam, faulting the country for \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  practices and invoking the same trade law the U.S. used in imposing sweeping tariffs on Chinese imports.\par \par \uc2 Trade experts say the \uc2 Office of the U.S. Trade Representative\uc2  aims to conclude the investigations into Vietnam so new tariffs can be unveiled before the end of the current administration.\par \par \uc2 William Reinsch\uc2 , a senior adviser at the Center for Strategic and International Studies, a nonpartisan think tank, called Wednesday's announcement a "34-day headache" for the named countries that could end when the Biden administration begins. "I think there is optimism on the part of other countries that Biden will handle this differently."\par \par \uc2 The U.S. has only ever labeled three countries as \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  manipulators. It previously labeled China a manipulator last year, removing the label in January, and from 1992 to 1994. Before that it had also hit Japan and Taiwan with the label in the late 1980s.\par \par \uc2 In the latest report, the Treasury urged China to improve transparency related to its exchange-rate management, particularly \b \highlight22\uc2 foreign exchange intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2 . It also said Beijing should increase the public understanding of the relationship between the \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  and the \b \highlight22\uc2 foreign-exchange\b0 \highlight\uc2  activities of state-owned banks, including the use of \b \highlight22\uc2 foreign-exchange\b0 \highlight\uc2  derivatives and activities in the offshore renminbi market.\par \par \uc2 --Yuka Hayashi contributed to this article.\par \par \uc2 Write to Kate Davidson at kate.davidson@wsj.com\par \par \uc2 (END) Dow Jones Newswires\par \par \uc2 December 17, 2020 02:32 ET (07:32 GMT)\par \par \uc2 Dokument DJDN000020201217egch000ln\par }\page {\par\fs20{\*\shppict{\pict\pngblip\picw172\pich40\picscaley100\picscalex100\picwgoal2580\pichgoal600
