\par\par \b \uc2 SE Asia Stocks-Trade subdued ahead of holidays, WHO meeting on virus outbreak\b0\par\par\uc2 507 \uc2 W\u246\'00\'F6rter\par \uc2 22 Januar 2020\par 05:39\par \uc2 Reuters News\par \uc2 LBA\par \uc2 Englisch\par \uc2 Copyright 2020 Thomson Reuters. All Rights Reserved. \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par     * Indonesia cenbank targets higher growth in 2020\par     * \f28 \uc2 \f2 EU\f28 \uc2 \f2  lawmakers back trade deal with Vietnam\par     * Healthcare stocks help Malaysia\par \par     By Soumyajit Saha\par     Jan 22 (Reuters) - Most Southeast Asian stock markets were\par little changed on Wednesday, while trading was thin as investors\par stayed on the sidelines ahead of Lunar New Year holidays and a\par \f28 \uc2 \f2 World Health Organisation (WHO) \f28 \uc2 \f2 meeting on the virus outbreak in\par China.\par     The WHO will consider whether to designate the outbreak an\par international public health emergency. Chinese officials said\par 440 people were confirmed infected, with nine deaths.\par  \par     Markets are watching for further developments in the fight\par to contain the coronavirus and any decision by the WHO will\par affect risk sentiment, KGI Securities' analyst Joel Ng said.\par     Indonesian stocks snapped two sessions of losses,\par helped by gains in financials.\par     \f28 \uc2 \f2 Bank Central Asia\f28 \uc2 \f2  and \f28 \uc2 \f2 Bank Mandiri (Persero)\par  \f28 \uc2 \f2 gained 0.9% and 0.7%, respectively.\par     The country's \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 central bank\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  said it targets the economy to\par grow at 5.1% to 5.5% this year, a slight increase from 2019, and\par will continue with accommodative monetary policy to support\par growth.\par     Separately, a senior \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 central bank\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  official said it was\par prepared to support stability of the rupiah \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 currency\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2 \par through market \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 intervention\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  amid heightened risk from the virus\par outbreak in China.\par     \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 Vietnam\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2 's benchmark touched a two-month high as financial\par shares rallied.\par     Joint Stock \f28 \uc2 \f2 Commercial Bank for Investment and Development\par of Vietnam\f28 \uc2 \f2  rose 0.7% and \f28 \uc2 \f2 Vietnam Joint Stock Commercial\par Bank for Industry and Trade\f28 \uc2 \f2  gained 1.4%.\par     \f28 \uc2 \f2 European Union\f28 \uc2 \f2  lawmakers gave initial backing on Tuesday for\par a free trade agreement struck with Vietnam, the bloc's most\par comprehensive such pact with a developing country. \par  \par     Malaysian stocks edged higher ahead of the \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 central\par bank\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2 's overnight policy rate decision, where it is expected to\par keep its benchmark interest rate unchanged, according to a\par Reuters poll.\par     Meanwhile, government data showed that December's consumer\par price index rose 1% from a year earlier, as expected, driven by\par expansion in all but two sectors tracked by the index.\par \par     Hospital operator IHH Healthcare rose 2.5%, while\par medical device maker \f28 \uc2 \f2 Top Glove Corp\f28 \uc2 \f2  was 5.7% higher.\par                 \par For Asian Companies click;  \par \par SOUTHEAST ASIAN STOCK MARKETS AS AT 0318 GMT\par   STOCK MARKETS                                           \par   Change on the day                                       \par   Market               Current     Previous    Pct Move\par                                    close       \par   Singapore            3245.34     3247.17     -0.06\par   Bangkok              1576.11     1574.94     0.07\par   Manila               7452.23     7466.65     -0.19\par   Jakarta              6255.253    6238.153    0.27\par   Kuala Lumpur         1589.72     1587.33     0.15\par  Ho Chi Minh           988.95      986.37      0.26\p