\par\par \b \uc2 BUZZ-COMMENT-Asia c. banks ignore yellow card from \uc2 US Treasury\b0\par\par\uc2 231 \uc2 W\u246\'00\'F6rter\par \uc2 29 Mai 2019\par 03:11\par \uc2 Reuters News\par \uc2 LBA\par \uc2 Englisch\par \uc2 Copyright 2019 Thomson Reuters. All Rights Reserved. \par \par \uc2 The \uc2 US Treasury\uc2  reached the same conclusion as it did in its October semi-annual report to Congress: that no major trading partners are manipulating their \b \highlight22\uc2 currencies\b0 \highlight\uc2 . But it highlighted six Asian countries for more scrutiny - China, Japan, South Korea, Malaysia, Singapore, and \b \highlight22\uc2 Vietnam\b0 \highlight\uc2 . The report said China's \b \highlight22\uc2 intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2  has been limited over the past year . The crux of the issue is that the US doesn't want trading partners to intentionally devalue their \b \highlight22\uc2 currencies\b0 \highlight\uc2  for export advantages, but in reality, no one's doing that. Instead, Asian central banks have been busy over the past month preventing their \b \highlight22\uc2 currencies\b0 \highlight\uc2  from falling too fast. China's persistent management of the daily USD/CNY benchmark is one example, but also South Korean authorities' suspected selling of USD in the spot market to bolster the KRW. The \b \highlight22\uc2 Monetary Authority\b0 \highlight\uc2  of Singapore\uc2  has also likely been active recently, keeping USD/SGD rallies in check.\par \par \uc2 Malaysia's \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  is well-known to restrain USD/MYR, sometimes through draconian measures involving deployment of monitoring personnel at banks' trading desks. Indeed, the US may be right about \b \highlight22\uc2 FX\b0 \highlight\uc2  manipulation, but it's clear why they're not about to hand out red cards to Asian central banks - they're all playing on the same side.\par \par \uc2 (Editing by Sam Holmes)\par \par \uc2 Released: 2019-6-1T01:11:15.000Z \par \par \uc2 D