\par\par \b \uc2 UPDATE 2-Vietnam devalues dong to protect exports, offset China's yuan action\b0\par\par\uc2 534 \uc2 W\u246\'00\'F6rter\par \uc2 19 August 2015\par 09:06\par \uc2 Reuters News\par \uc2 LBA\par \uc2 Englisch\par \uc2 Copyright 2015 Thomson Reuters. All Rights Reserved. \par \par \uc2 * Vietnam lowers mid-point rate to 21,890 dong/dollar\par \par \uc2 * Trading band widened to 3 pct from 2 pct\par \par \uc2 * \b \highlight22\uc2 Intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2  timely and warranted - economists\par \par \uc2 * Adjustments sufficient till early 2016 -c.bank (Adds details, more comments)\par \par \uc2 By Ho Binh Minh and My Pham\par \par \uc2 HANOI, Aug 19 (Reuters) - Vietnam devalued the dong on Wednesday for the third time this year as authorities moved to bolster a languid export sector facing fresh challenges from a surprise devaluation of the Chinese yuan.\par \par \uc2 The \uc2 State Bank of \b \highlight22\uc2 Vietnam\b0 \highlight\uc2  (SBV) said the \b \highlight22\uc2 intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2  was also in anticipation of the \uc2 U.S. Federal Reserve\uc2  raising rates. It widened the dollar/dong trading band for the second time in a week, underscoring concerns a weaker yuan could further inflame bloated trade deficit.\par \par \uc2 Vietnam's economy is closely tied to its communist neighbour, with three quarters of bilateral trade worth $60 billion being imports from China.\par \par \uc2 Global markets were alarmed when China devalued the yuan by nearly 2 percent on Aug. 11, with analysts fearing a further weakening over coming months and heightened worries of a global \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  war.\par \par \uc2 Vietnam lowered the official mid-point rate by 0.99 percent to 21,890 dong per dollar and widened the trading band for the second time in six days, to 3 percent from 2 percent. The SBV allowed 1-percent \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  depreciations in January and May.\par \par \uc2 ANZ analysts said the devaluation was more aggressive than expected, while HSBC Vietnam welcomed the quick response.\par \par \uc2 "The move of the \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  is fast and almost unprecedented in Vietnam," \uc2 HSBC\uc2  Vietnam chairman Pham Hong Hai said in a statement. "The bank is ready to handle market challenges."\par \par \uc2 The dong, among Asia's most resilient \b \highlight22\uc2 currencies\b0 \highlight\uc2 , dropped to 22,380/22,400 per dollar on the interbank market at 0344 GMT on Wednesday, off 1.3 percent from the previous day and down 4.5 percent so far this year. Gold rose 1.3 percent to sell at 34.62 million dong ($1,547) per tael in Hanoi.\par \par \uc2 TRADE CHALLENGE\par \par \uc2 The weaker yuan has sparked concern of more Chinese goods flooding Vietnam's market. Trade with China was in deficit of $19.33 billion in the first seven months of 2015, versus a $14.88 billion deficit a year ago.\par \par \uc2 Export-reliant Vietnam saw shipments grow 8.9 percent in that period, below the government's 10 percent target.\par \par \uc2 The SBV said the dong's exchange rate had "sufficiently large ground" to be flexible into next year.\par \par \uc2 Cheaper Chinese exports could pose a challenge as Vietnam's low-cost manufacturing sector competes with China for orders for clothing brands and electronics firms like \uc2 Samsung\uc2  and \uc2 Microsoft\uc2 , which has reduced \b \highlight22\uc2 operations\b0 \highlight\uc2  in China and shifted some to Vietnam.\par \par \uc2 With Wednesday's new band, the dong could fall to 22,547 per dollar in interbank deals, or 2 percent down from the previous day. The dong has now weakened 4.5 percent in interbank and 3 percent on unofficial markets, against the SBV's pledge to let it slip 2 percent in 2015. (Editing by Martin Petty & Shri Navaratnam) \par \par \uc2 Released: 2015-8-19T08:06:47.000Z \par \par \uc2 Dokument LBA0000020150819eb8j004mq\par }\page {\par\fs20{\*\shppict{\pict\pngblip\picw172\pich40\picscaley100\picscalex100\picwgoal2580\pichgoal600
