\par\par \b \uc2 Vietnam's  \highlight22\uc2 forex \highlight\uc2  reserves at more than $35 bln -deputy prime minister\b0\par\par\uc2 138 \uc2 W\u246\'00\'F6rter\par \uc2 13 Juni 2015\par 05:53\par \uc2 Reuters News\par \uc2 LBA\par \uc2 Englisch\par \uc2 Copyright 2015 Thomson Reuters. All Rights Reserved.   \par \par \uc2 HANOI, June 13 (Reuters) - Vietnam's \b \highlight22\uc2 forex\b0 \highlight\uc2  reserves now stand at more than $35 billion, equivalent to more than 12 weeks of imports, deputy prime minister Nguyen Xuan Phuc told the National Assembly in a televised session.\par \par \uc2 "It's not too much...so we will seek expertise from representatives and international economists to best use this fund to govern the macroeconomy and to \b \highlight22\uc2 intervene\b0 \highlight\uc2  in the \b \highlight22\uc2 forex\b0 \highlight\uc2  market when necessary," Phuc said.\par \par \b \highlight22\uc2 Vietnam\b0 \highlight\uc2  has devalued the dong \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  twice this year, by a combined 2 percent, to stabilise the \b \highlight22\uc2 foreign exchange\b0 \highlight\uc2  market. Last December, the \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  said dong depreciation would be less than 2 percent for the whole of 2015. (Reporting by \uc2 Mai Nguyen\uc2 ; Editing by Clarence Fernandez) \par \par \uc2 Released: 2015-6-13T04:53:20.000Z   \par \par \uc2 Dokument LBA0000020150613eb6d004ed\par }\page {\par\fs20{\*\shppict{\pict\pngblip\picw172\pich40\picscaley100\picscalex100\picwgoal2580\pichgoal600
