\par\par \b \uc2 UPDATE 1-Vietnam ready to sell  \highlight22\uc2 forex \highlight\uc2  to stabilise exchange rate - c.bank\b0\par\par\uc2 456 \uc2 W\u246\'00\'F6rter\par \uc2 27 Mai 2015\par 06:00\par \uc2 Reuters News\par \uc2 LBA\par \uc2 Englisch\par \uc2 Copyright 2015 Thomson Reuters. All Rights Reserved. \par \par \uc2 (Changes attribution, adds details, background)\par \par \uc2 By Ho Binh Minh\par \par \uc2 HANOI, May 27 (Reuters) - \uc2 Vietnam's \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  stands ready to help stabilise the dollar/dong exchange rate and will keep the 2-percent band movement for the rest of the year, a deputy governor said.\par \par \uc2 The \uc2 State Bank of \b \highlight22\uc2 Vietnam\b0 \highlight\uc2  (SBV) "is ready to sell foreign \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  for \b \highlight22\uc2 intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2  to keep the exchange rate stable within the band committed to at the start of the year", Nguyen Thi Hong said in an interview posted on Wednesday on the \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2 's website (sbv.gov.vn).\par \par \uc2 The \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2 , which does not regularly report \b \highlight22\uc2 foreign-exchange\b0 \highlight\uc2  reserves, said last year the total in June 2014 was $35 billion, compared with $9 billion in 2011.\par \par \uc2 The SBV, which has committed to keeping the Vietnamese dong move within a 2 percent band against the dollar in 2015, on May 7 devalued the dong for the second time this year.\par \par \uc2 The devaluations were intended to spur exports and curb imports. On Tuesday, the government announced a $3 billion deficit for the first five months. In the same period of 2014, there was a $1.52 billion surplus.\par \par \uc2 "Standing by the view of the overall national interest, the SBV will continue its direction for the whole of 2015 to regulate (the exchange rate) within a 2-percent band," Hong said.\par \par \uc2 On Tuesday, the dong fell to 21,860 per dollar on Vietnam's interbank market, or 0.98 percent weaker than its 21,645 level right after the May 7 devaluation.\par \par \uc2 UNCHANGED MIDPOINT\par \par \uc2 On Wednesday, the dong strengthened to 21,765/21,825 per dollar on the interbank market. The \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  has kept the official mid-point rate unchanged at 21,673 dong per dollar since May 7.\par \par \uc2 Dollar/dong transactions are allowed to move in a band of 1 percent around the mid-point set daily.\par \par \uc2 A substantial dong devaluation could benefit exporters but it will also bring negative impact to importers and raise the sovereign debt, Hong said.\par \par \uc2 Keeping a strong domestic \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  will also encourage banks to sell dollar to banks, which has been a practice since the start of this year, she added.\par \par \uc2 In a report on May 8, right after the second devaluation, ANZ said the dong could fall 3 percent to 22,050 dong per dollar at the end of 2015, faster than a 1.4-percent depreciation last year.\par \par \uc2 The government has projected that there could be a $6 billion trade deficit this year. For the past three years, there have been trade surpluses. (Reporting by Ho Binh Minh; Editing by Richard Borsuk) \par \par \uc2 Released: 2015-5-27T05:00:42.000Z \par \par \uc2 Dokument LBA0000020150527eb5r0066c\par }\page {\par\fs20{\*\shppict{\pict\pngblip\picw172\pich40\picscaley100\picscalex100\picwgoal2580\pichgoal600
