\par\par \b \uc2 Vietnam c.bank to keep  \highlight22\uc2 forex \highlight\uc2  rate stable\b0\par\par\uc2 311 \uc2 W\u246\'00\'F6rter\par \uc2 6 Dezember 2013\par 11:36\par \uc2 Reuters News\par \uc2 LBA\par \uc2 Englisch\par \uc2 (c) 2013 Reuters Limited   \par \par \uc2 HANOI, Dec 6 (Reuters) - Vietnam's \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  said on Friday it would keep the dollar/dong exchange rate stable until the end of this year, a move likely to allay investor concerns about a devaluation of the dong \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2 .\par \par \uc2 The \uc2 State Bank of \b \highlight22\uc2 Vietnam\b0 \highlight\uc2  (SBV) saw no reason to \b \highlight22\uc2 intervene\b0 \highlight\uc2  because the country's macro economy and money markets, which it said were performing positively.\par \par \uc2 The dong rose to 21,120/21,160 per dollar on the interbank market on Friday, or 0.24 percent up from Monday.\par \par \uc2 "The exchange rate movements recently have been mainly driven by psychological factors," SBV said in a statement.\par \par \uc2 "There is no reason for an adjustment of the exchange rate and the SBV will not change the rates from now until end of 2013."\par \par \uc2 The \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  weakened the dong by 1 percent against the dollar in June in what it said was to accurately reflect supply/demand on foreign \b \highlight22\uc2 currencies\b0 \highlight\uc2 .\par \par \uc2 Friday's announcement is likely to be welcomed by investors, many of whom had expected a devaluation at the end of this year, said Nguyen Phong, a macro economy specialist at Viet Capital Securities.\par \par \uc2 "A stable \b \highlight22\uc2 foreign exchange\b0 \highlight\uc2  rate would allow foreign investors to extend their buying in the share market," he said.\par \par \uc2 The rise in foreign reserves and decrease in the ratio of dollarisation, a longstanding problem for Vietnam's policymakers, have eased pressure on the SBV to devalue the dong.\par \par \uc2 SBV was holding $25 billion in foreign reserves as at September 2013 and the country's dollarisation had fallen to 12 percent at the end of August this year, from 15.8 percent at the end of 2011, state media reported. (Reporting by Nguyen Phuong Linh and \uc2 Mai Nguyen\uc2 ; Editing by Martin Petty)\par \par \uc2 VIETNAM-ECONOMY/ | SI | ABN | M | D | DNP   \par \par \uc2 Dokument LBA0000020131206e9c6000if\par }\page {\par\fs20{\*\shppict{\pict\pngblip\picw184\pich22\picscaley100\picscalex100\picwgoal2760\pichgoal330
