\par\par \b \uc2 UPDATE: Vietnam  \highlight22\uc2 Central Bank \highlight\uc2  To Sell Dollars As Devaluation Ruled Out\b0\par\par\uc2 646 \uc2 W\u246\'00\'F6rter\par \uc2 4 November 2010\par 12:19\par \uc2 Dow Jones Global Equities News\par \uc2 DJI\par \uc2 Englisch\par \uc2 (c) 2010 Dow Jones & Company, Inc.  \par \par \uc2 (Combines previous stories, adds details on \b \highlight22\uc2 FX\b0 \highlight\uc2  rate, background)\par \par \uc2 \f2 \par  \par    By Nguyen Pham Muoi \par    Of DOW JONES NEWSWIRES \par  \f28 \par \par \uc2 HANOI (Dow Jones)--Vietnam's \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  said Thursday it will sell U.S. dollars to boost liquidity in the local market amid increasing demand from importers and growing pressure on the sliding dong, as an official ruled out a further devaluation this year.\par \par \uc2 Vietnam is expected to see an increased supply of foreign \b \highlight22\uc2 currencies\b0 \highlight\uc2  during the last two months because of disbursements of large foreign loans, foreign investment and remittances, the \uc2 State Bank of Vietnam\uc2  said in a statement.\par \par \uc2 "Pressure on demand (for the greenback) is expected to also fall because of the ability to curb trade deficits during the last months of the year," the statement said.\par \par \uc2 The country is forecast to have a balanced current account this year, it noted, adding that the \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  will continue to maintain the dollar's exchange rate at current levels.\par \par \uc2 The dollar, whose exchange rate was set at VND18,932 Thursday, has risen more than 7% in the last month, prompting a significant increase in the price of various goods and commodities.\par \par \uc2 With inflation also remaining stubbornly high, the government has been under growing pressure to further devalue its local unit.\par \par \uc2 Le Duc Thuy, who is chairman of the National Committee of Financial Supervision, was quoted by state media as saying the government has decided not to devalue the dong between now and the Lunar New Year. Vietnam has devalued the dong three times since last November.\par \par \uc2 Dollar rates in the market have risen far above the official rates issued by the \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2 , which have a negative impact on the economy, Thuy noted.\par \par \uc2 Thuy also said the government has decided not to ask banks to lower interest rates on dong deposits, which it hopes will boost the value of the local \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  as more people hold dong deposits instead of dollars.\par \par \uc2 "Government officials met Wednesday evening to decide those two key measures, among others, to stabilize the monetary market and macroeconomic conditions," financial news website Vinacorp.vn quoted Thuy as saying.\par \par \uc2 "The \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  will sell dollars to commercial banks to fully meet every necessary demand (from the market)," he said.\par \par \uc2 He added that while Vietnam's \b \highlight22\uc2 foreign exchange\b0 \highlight\uc2  reserves have fallen from a previous high of $23 billion, "the current volume remains relatively large enough for the \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  to \b \highlight22\uc2 intervene\b0 \highlight\uc2  into the market."\par \par \uc2 Hanoi-based bankers estimate \b \highlight22\uc2 Vietnam\b0 \highlight\uc2 's current \b \highlight22\uc2 forex\b0 \highlight\uc2  reserves at $10 billion, equivalent to seven weeks of imports.\par \par \uc2 The Thoi Bao Kinh Te newspaper reported that Vietnam's trade deficit this year is expected at $12 billion, lower than a previous forecast of $14 billion, which would help narrow the current account deficit to $4 billion this year from $8.8 billion last year.\par \par \uc2 The \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  bought a net $300 million in September and sold a net $200 million in October, Thuy said, indicating the \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank intervened\b0 \highlight\uc2  in markets to smooth the dong's movements.\par \par \uc2 With immediate effect, local banks will free to set their interest rates within certain ranges--with deposit rates to be between 12% and 13%, and lending rates between 13% and 15%, Thuy said.\par \par \uc2 The rates for dong deposits are higher than the current rate of 11%.\par \par \uc2 "The rise of the interest rates for the dong will boost faith in the value of the dong," he said, adding that higher rates will mean tighter monetary policy, which will help rein in high inflation.\par \par \uc2 The government's figures showed Vietnam's consumer price index rose 9.66% on-year and 1.05% on-month in October, and state media forecast inflation to hit double digits later this year.\par \par \uc2 -By Nguyen Pham Muoi, Dow Jones Newswires; 84-4-35123041; phammuoi.nguyen@dowjones.com [ 04-11-10 1119GMT ]\par \par \uc2 Dokument DJI0000020101104e6b40017s\par }\page {\par\fs20{\*\shppict{\pict\pngblip\picw172\pich40\picscaley100\picscalex100\picwgoal2580\pichgoal600
