\par\par \b \uc2 UPDATE 1-Vietnam to use reserves, won't devalue -media\b0\par\par\uc2 362 \uc2 W\u246\'00\'F6rter\par \uc2 4 November 2010\par 06:40\par \uc2 Reuters News\par \uc2 LBA\par \uc2 Englisch\par \uc2 (c) 2010 Reuters Limited   \par \par \uc2 (Adds details)\par \par \uc2 HANOI, Nov 4 (Reuters) - Vietnam will use its \b \highlight22\uc2 foreign exchange\b0 \highlight\uc2  reserves to cover a shortage of dollars which is sharply pushing down the dong, but will not devalue its \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  at least through early 2011, a state media said on Thursday.\par \par \uc2 The government has requested the \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  to conduct "strong enough \b \highlight22\uc2 intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2 " and inject dollars to stabilise the market, the \b \highlight22\uc2 Vietnam\b0 \highlight\uc2  Economic Times quoted Le Duc Thuy, Chairman of the National Financial Supervisory Commission, as saying in an online report.\par \par \uc2 The shortage of dollars, caused by strong demand from businesses, has been putting heavy downward pressure on the dong, which was trading around 21,000 to a dollar from around 20,450 on Monday.\par \par \uc2 But state broadcaster \uc2 Vietnam Television\uc2  (VTV) quoted Thuy as saying the \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  will neither widen the trading band nor change the official \b \highlight22\uc2 foreign exchange\b0 \highlight\uc2  rate until the lunar new year, which will start in early 2011.\par \par \uc2 Thuy was speaking on behalf of the government, the VTV said.\par \par \uc2 The government said another devaluation at this point in time is not advantageous and may have negative impacts on inflation, the online report quoted Thuy as saying.\par \par \uc2 Vietnam's \b \highlight22\uc2 foreign exchange\b0 \highlight\uc2  reserves have shrunk, but it is still large enough to stabilise the market, it cited Thuy as saying.\par \par \uc2 The reserves is now equivalent to six or seven weeks of import, the report said.\par \par \uc2 The International Monetary Fund said in September Vietnam's foreign reserves had been growing after a sharp drop.\par \par \uc2 It estimated that international reserves, including gold, would grow to $15.4 billion by the end of 2010 and $19.2 billion next year, from $14.1 billion at the end of 2009.\par \par \uc2 Vietnam's \b \highlight22\uc2 foreign exchange\b0 \highlight\uc2  reserves dropped sharply from 2008 through the middle of this year as foreign \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  inflows flagged during the global slowdown and Vietnamese switched to dollars and gold fearing the worst for their own \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2 . ($1=19,480 dong) (Reporting by Ngo Thi Ngoc Chau and John Ruwitch; Editing by Kazunori Takada)\par \par \uc2 VIETNAM-ECONOMY/DOLLAR (UPDATE 1)|LANGEN|ABN|M|E|RBN|D|SI|RNP|DNP   \par \par \uc2 Dokument LBA0000020101104e6b4000dz\par }\page {\par\fs20{\*\shppict{\pict\pngblip\picw172\pich40\picscaley100\picscalex100\picwgoal2580\pichgoal600
