\par\par \b \uc2 UPDATE 2- \highlight22\uc2 Vietnam \highlight\uc2  c.bank ready to  \highlight22\uc2 intervene \highlight\uc2  heavily on dong\b0\par\par\uc2 800 \uc2 W\u246\'00\'F6rter\par \uc2 3 Dezember 2009\par 04:27\par \uc2 Reuters News\par \uc2 LBA\par \uc2 Englisch\par \uc2 (c) 2009 Reuters Limited   \par \par \uc2  * Comments come after last week's \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  devaluation\par \par \uc2  * Vietcombank: $2 bln in \b \highlight22\uc2 intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2  would stabilise market\par \par \uc2  * Some analysts sceptical sustained \b \highlight22\uc2 intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2  possible  (Adds markets, reaction)\par \par \uc2  By John Ruwitch\par \par \uc2  HANOI, Dec 3 (Reuters) - \b \highlight22\uc2 Vietnam\b0 \highlight\uc2 's \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  is prepared to \b \highlight22\uc2 intervene\b0 \highlight\uc2  on a "very big scale" to stabilise the beleaguered dong <VND=>, backed by \b \highlight22\uc2 foreign exchange\b0 \highlight\uc2  reserves of more than $16 billion, a deputy governor said on Thursday.\par \par \uc2  The comments by Nguyen Van Binh come a week after Vietnam devalued the dong for the third time since the start of 2008 to relieve pressure on the \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  in the face of dollar hoarding. [ID:nBKK78305]\par \par \uc2  The head of a major Vietnamese bank said $2 billion in \b \highlight22\uc2 intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2  might stabilise the dong, but others were sceptical the \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  had sufficient reserves to allow sustained \b \highlight22\uc2 intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2 .\par \par \uc2  While the closely managed \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  was quoted on Thursday within the dollar/dong trading band set by the \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2 , the unofficial rate remained well outside the band.\par \par \uc2  "We are ready to carry out very big scale (\b \highlight22\uc2 intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2 ) in order to keep the exchange rate stable in the market," the deputy governor of the \uc2 State Bank of Vietnam\uc2  said.\par \par \uc2  "It will depend on the real condition in the market. We can sell as much as we need in order to make the market stable," Binh told reporters.\par \par \uc2  "According to our figures, frankly speaking, our reserves are more than three months of imports. That is consistent with international standards," he said. "I think they are more than $16 billion."\par \par \uc2  The International Monetary Fund said on Thursday reserves were below two-and-a-half months of imports, but the reason for the different estimates was not immediately clear.\par \par \uc2  Last week, the \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  shifted the mid point it sets for daily trading down by more than 5 percent overnight and reduced the trading band of the dong to 3 percent either side of the mid point from 5 percent.\par \par \uc2  The \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  also said it would call on exporters to sell it their \b \highlight22\uc2 foreign exchange\b0 \highlight\uc2  to help relieve the dollar shortage.\par \par \uc2  In remarks prepared for Vietnam's annual donors' meeting on Thursday, Binh said those were a "first step".\par \par \uc2  The government set that policy in motion by ordering the big exporters, including monopolies \uc2 Petrovietnam\uc2  and Vinacomin, to sell their dollars to the \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2 .\par \par \uc2  NORMAL STATUS FOR $2 BILLION\par \par \uc2  The chief executive of Vietcombank, which is responsible for ensuring \b \highlight22\uc2 foreign exchange\b0 \highlight\uc2  supplies for the country's trade payments, said \b \highlight22\uc2 intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2  could work.\par \par \uc2  "If the State Bank sells around $2 billion, the market will surely return to normal status," Nguyen Phuoc Thanh was quoted as saying in the official Thanh Nien daily.\par \par \uc2  However, Matt Hildebrandt, an economist at \uc2 JPMorgan\uc2  in Singapore, said the level of reserves suggested limited room for \b \highlight22\uc2 intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2 .\par \par \uc2  "If it gets below three months, it's a red flag," he said. "Reserves are probably a little bit lower than you'd like in order to feel comfortable, but they are not low enough to say that Vietnam is on the verge of a crisis."\par \par \uc2  At the end of September, only Pakistan and Bangladesh had smaller reserves than Vietnam among the Asian countries monitored regularly by Reuters.\par \par \uc2  Indeed, a treasurer at a foreign bank in Vietnam said the deputy governor's comments were likely no more than verbal \b \highlight22\uc2 intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2 .\par \par \uc2  "Continuous \b \highlight22\uc2 intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2  is not sustainable given the continuing current account deficit," he said, declining to be identified because he is not authorised to speak to the media.\par \par \uc2  Previous attempts to control a long-term slide in the dong included two devaluations last year and widening the \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2 's trading band several times.\par \par \uc2  On Thursday, the dong was quoted in the official market at 18,473 per dollar, 2.85 percent below the mid point.\par \par \uc2  In unofficial markets, the dong was quoted at 19,350, more than 7 percent below the mid point, although it was stronger than levels the day before the devaluation was announced.\par \par \uc2  Dollar hoarding is widespread in Vietnam among ordinary people and companies alike.\par \par \uc2  Many big-ticket business transactions are conducted in dollars, but their supply has been squeezed by the global downturn, which hit \b \highlight22\uc2 foreign exchange\b0 \highlight\uc2  earners such as exports, remittances and foreign investment.\par \par \uc2  Two devaluations of the dong in 2008 had also fed expectations for yet further devaluations.  (Additional reporting by Ho Binh Minh and Nguyen Nhat Lam; Writing by Neil Fullick; Editing by Kim Coghill)  ((john.ruwitch@thomsonreuters.com; +84 4 3825 9623; Reuters Messaging: john.ruwitch.reuters.com@reuters.net))  ((If you have a query or comment on this story, send an email to news.feedback.asia@thomsonreuters.com)) \par \par \uc2 VIETNAM-ECONOMY/RESERVES (UPDATE 2)|LANGEN|ABN|M|E|RBN|D|SI|RNP|DNP   \par \par \uc2 Dokument LBA0000020091203e5c300058\par }\page {\par\fs20{\*\shppict{\pict\pngblip\picw184\pich22\picscaley100\picscalex100\picwgoal2760\pichgoal330
