\par\par \b \uc2 MARKET TALK:  \highlight22\uc2 Vietnam \highlight\uc2  Shares Now +3.7% On Watchdog  \highlight22\uc2 Intervention \highlight\b0\par\par\uc2 1,675 \uc2 W\u246\'00\'F6rter\par \uc2 27 November 2009\par 03:55\par \uc2 Dow Jones Capital Markets Report\par \uc2 CM\par \uc2 Englisch\par \uc2 (c) 2009 Dow Jones & Company, Inc.   \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par  \par Contact us in Singapore. 65 64154 140; \par MarketTalk@dowjones.com \par  \par  \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 0255 GMT [Dow Jones] Vietnam shares +3.7% at 499.62, swinging to positive territory (after falling over 4% to 462 earlier on subsidized loan program exit report); players coming back to buy after the State Securities Commission told brokerage firms to stop asking investors to offload shares on margin calls. "The watchdog authority just held an urgent meeting with all the brokerage firms to urge them to prevent the market from further falling, and this \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 intervention\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  has been really helpful," says analyst with Sacombank Securities. Volume moderate at 45 million shares valued at VND1.8 trillion. Among advancers, Vietinbank (CTG.VH) +4.7% at VND26,300, Vietcombank (VCB.VH) +4.8% at VND43,800, Hoa Phat Group (HPG.VH) +5% at VND63,000.(HAA)\f28 \par \par \uc2 0253 GMT [Dow Jones] Kospi last down 2.6% at 1558.64 in light volume, extending losses led by banks, construction firms on foreign selling amid fears unfolding Dubai debt crisis may spread to European financial firms. "I don't know the exact size of European banks' exposure to Dubai debts, but European investors may need to deleverage and sell some local stocks (to secure cash) if European financial firms are shown to have large exposure (to Dubai debts)," says Lee Sun-yup at Shinhan Investment Corporation; foreigners now net sellers of shares worth KRW139.5 billion, 6,900 futures contracts so far. Adds "I'm worried this negative issue from Dubai may hurt consumer sentiment, preventing a visible U.S. retail sales recovery" during much-awaited year-end shopping season. Eyes whether Kospi recovers above 120-day moving average around 1560 before ending today. KB Financial (105560.SE) down 4.5% at KRW58,000, \uc2 Woori Finance\uc2  (053000.SE) down 7.0% at KRW14,00; GS Engineering (006360.SE) down 7.4% at KRW106,000, \uc2 Samsung C&T Corp\uc2  (000830.SE) down 2.5% at KRW46,100.(SKS)\par \par \uc2 0248 GMT [Dow Jones] \uc2 Barclays Capital\uc2  says ongoing quick unwinding in risk trades should be contained by policymakers before it becomes \uc2 Lehman Brothers\uc2 -like mess, but may cause some damage in the meantime. Notes concerns about Dubai World's debt payment freeze, "and to a lesser extent Vietnam", so far resulting in classic risk-off configuration - with JPY, USD as best performers, AUD, ZAR and NZD the worst, and EUR also under pressure. "The key question is whether this will be a replay of 4Q 2008 or be a shorter, more benign correction. The key difference between now and then is that policymakers are much more on guard...now the odds are likely that they will respond forcefully to prevent an extended period of market disruption."(DRB)\par \par \uc2 0235 GMT [Dow Jones] NZD/USD taking beating after markets spooked by news Dubai World asked for more time to meet its debt obligations. Bank of NZ \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  strategist Danica Hampton says not clear if markets are overreacting; "There's been a knee jerk reaction that it's all bad news. The question now is how much exposure do people really have to Dubai. We need to know that in order to know how long lasting the effect is going to be." Says Kiwi likely to remain under pressure as markets increasingly worried about how global economy will cope if policy makers around world remove stimulus. Investors will be looking to U.S. equity markets later in global day for direction though trading likely to be muted after Thanksgiving Holiday. "The moral of the story is that \b \highlight22\uc2 currencies\b0 \highlight\uc2  like the Kiwi and the Aussie will remain a sell on rallies." Pair last 0.7083, after dipping to 0.7051, lowest since Oct 2. (RSH)\par \par \uc2 0231 GMT [Dow Jones] Volatilities implied by 1-month ATM USD/JPY options mark 7-week high at 13.90%/14.60% from 11.10%/11.80% in Tokyo yesterday; spot slide to fresh 14-year low at 84.82 fuels demand for hedges against further falls. If any materialize later in global day, vols may rise by another percentage point by early next week as investors bargain hunt USD-put options, still at relatively low levels, dealers say.(ATM)\par \par \uc2 0229 GMT [Dow Jones] Spot gold at $1,187.90/oz, up $5.15 vs London PM kerb. News on Dubai World debt freeze, resulting risk aversion and investors' move to USD not having much impact as gold "has a life of its own now," says Sydney-based trader. While causing market jitters, Dubai World isn't of great concern for global financial system, says trader. Gold moving up in all \b \highlight22\uc2 currencies\b0 \highlight\uc2 , including commodity \b \highlight22\uc2 currencies\b0 \highlight\uc2  like AUD, where gold is up around A$1,303/oz vs A$1,230/oz last week. Gold's usual inverse correlation with USD weaker at present, but should investors move back into greenback on larger scale, gold will suffer, says NAB commodities economist Ben Westmore. Adds gold supply-demand fundamentals also asserting themselves given falling output, investors and retail demand into ETFs, \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  buying. "Whereas in the past the focus was on gold as a financial instrument, changes in the fundamental market balance are now important," says Westmore. (EFB)\par \par \uc2 0227 GMT [Dow Jones] HSI down 3.0% at 21,544.57, off intraday low of 21,458.01, lowest level since Nov. 5, due to sharp falls in financial stocks, with \uc2 HSBC\uc2  (0005.HK) down 5.9% at HK$88.60 on Dubai crisis. Linus Yip at First Shanghai says index may test next psychological support of 21,000 near-term, as Dubai crisis triggers concerns for financial stocks ahead, he says. "Dubai crisis shouldn't be comparable to \uc2 Lehman Brothers\uc2 ', but this is not a good sign, as it indicates that the global financial tsunami has yet to end," Yip says. On China banks, Yip says impact from Dubai should be small, as they have very minimal exposure to the region. "But these stocks remain top targets for shorts on fundraising concerns, and I expect more falls in China banks ahead," he adds. ICBC (1398.HK) down 2.7% at HK$6.43, Bank of China (3988.HK) down 3.2% at HK$4.21. Volume strong at HK$15.18 billion.(SUT)\par \par \uc2 0222 GMT [Dow Jones] Taiwan financial stocks subindex down 3.1%, underperforming Taiex's 2.1% drop, on Dubai World news, says \uc2 Masterlink Securities\uc2  analyst Greg Ou. But expects downside limited as only few local financial firms likely to hold Dubai World related debt, also shares likely supported by expected signing of cross-Strait trade pact next year; "there has already been a correction in financial stocks after the signing of the cross-Strait financial MOU earlier this month." Chinatrust (2891.TW) down 4.1% at NT$18.80, Cathay (2882.TW) down 2.2% at NT$56.9, Shin Kong (2888.TW) down 5.1% at NT$13.10, First Financial (2892.TW) down 3.3% at NT$19.30.(WUM)\par \par \uc2 0218 GMT [Dow Jones] LME copper higher at $6,855/ton, up $30 vs PM kerb, metals market steady despite broader market jitters about Dubai World debt problems. "The Dubai news is important, but really, in the grand scheme of things, it's not that major," says Sydney-based trader. Rising stocks for LME copper, aluminum continue to feed doubt about high, rising prices. Trader tips LME copper stocks to rise to 700,000 tons in next three months, from over 400,000 tons now. "There is a disconnect but a lot of it is investor sentiment. China expectations keep getting revised higher," says NAB commodities economist Ben Westmore. Adds question marks remain over how much of China's rebounding demand is built on stimulus package, feeding imbalances in Chinese economy. "But people want exposure to China, and a lot of that is through commodities," says Westmore. (EFB)\par \par \uc2 0217 GMT [Dow Jones] USD/PHP higher on risk aversion sparked by Dubai World debt woes, but gains probably limited by USD inflows to Philippine corporates; pair last at 47.03 in light volume after settling 46.82 late yesterday; local bank trader tips trading range 46.80-47.20 on day. "We saw an initial gap up because of the Dubai story, but with little volume. Corporate inflows will likely cap the dollar's gain," trader says. (SFL)\par \par \uc2 0210 GMT [Dow Jones] Nikkei ends morning session down 1.8% at 9213.51; intraday low so far 9180.47 and further decline today may be limited as USD/JPY seems to have temporarily stabilized after brief drop below 85.00 earlier this morning, says Tachibana Securities market analyst Kenichi Hirano. Still, Nikkei may fall more next week and test technical support at July 13 intraday low of 9050.33, he says; "In addition to worries about further yen strengthening, the unfolding debt crisis in Dubai is bringing back fears over European financial markets." General contractors sharply lower on Nikkei report they have taken on major contracts tendered by Dubai World's property development unit; Shimizu (1803.TO) down 8.3% at Y277, Taisei (1803.TO) down 7.7% at Y144. 28/33 Topix subindexes lower, with iron & steel (down 3.3%), nonferrous metals (down 3.1%), construction (down 3.0%) pacing decline. \uc2 Honda Motor\uc2  (7267.TO) down 3.3% at Y2,675. \uc2 Sony\uc2  (6758.TO) down 3.0% at Y2,300. Canon (7751.TO) down 1.8% at Y3,230. (JUO)\par \par \uc2 0208 GMT [Dow Jones] Thai shares set to follow regional markets lower after European stocks sold off heavily on concerns over Dubai World debt. "While we believe the fundamental impact to Asia and Thailand's economy is limited, risk aversion will likely dominate the market," says KGI Securities strategist Rakpong Chaisuparakul; but adds doesn't expect free-fall, with some bargain hunting around technical support levels of 678, then 670. Any upside capped at 690. Market closed down 1.4% at 685.73 yesterday. (LHM) [ 11-26-09 2155ET ]\par \par \uc2 4022   \par \par \uc2 Dokument CM00000020091127e5br00022\par }\page {\par\fs20{\*\shppict{\pict\pngblip\picw172\pich40\picscaley100\picscalex100\picwgoal2580\pichgoal600
