\par\par \b \uc2 UPDATE 2-Vietnam to let dong dip 3 pct vs dollar on Dec 25\b0\par\par\uc2 By Ho Binh Minh   \par \uc2 663 \uc2 W\u246\'00\'F6rter\par \uc2 24 Dezember 2008\par 15:23\par \uc2 Reuters News\par \uc2 LBA\par \uc2 Englisch\par \uc2 (c) 2008 Reuters Limited   \par \par \uc2 (Adds quotes, background)\par \par \uc2 HANOI, Dec 24 (Reuters) - Vietnam's \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  will let the dong fall 3 percent against the dollar from Dec. 25 in interbank transactions, a state-run newspaper quoted Governor Nguyen Van Giau as saying on Wednesday.\par \par \uc2 The mid-point will be 16,988 dong per dollar on Thursday, from 16,494 dong on Wednesday, while the daily trading band remains unchanged at 3 percent either side of the mid-point, Giau was quoted by the Vietnam Economic Times newspaper as saying.\par \par \uc2 The State Bank of Vietnam's mid-point quote on Reuters <SBOV> late on Wednesday was 16,989 for Dec. 25.\par \par \uc2 The dong could fall to 17,497 per dollar on Thursday in interbank quotations, from 16,989 dong per dollar on Wednesday, Giau told a news conference in Ho Chi Minh City, to which foreign media were not invited.\par \par \uc2 The mid-point \b \highlight22\uc2 intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2  on Thursday by the State Bank of \b \highlight22\uc2 Vietnam\b0 \highlight\uc2  is the second this year after it allowed the rate to drop 2 percent in June. So far, the dong has fallen roughly 6 percent against the dollar this year on the interbank market.\par \par \uc2 The government has told the \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  "to flexibly adjust the exchange rate following market signals, encourage exports, control imports, stabilise the macroeconomy and strive to manage the international balance of payment without facing a deficit," Giau said.\par \par \uc2 The devaluation was not expected.\par \par \uc2 The interbank dollar/dong market has been frozen for weeks, with the dong stuck at the weak end of its band and almost no deals being done. Banks say they have been rationing dollars for long-term or special customers.\par \par \uc2 "Trade and investment flows have been negative, putting selling pressure on the dong, and recent bouts of inflation have also conspired to drive the \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2 's value lower," said Michael Woolfolk, senior \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  strategist at Bank of New York Mellon.\par \par \uc2 "The decision today merely reflects the supply and demand conditions that have built up in recent weeks."\par \par \uc2 In the past two weeks bankers in Vietnam had said the market was expecting the \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  to widen the band to 4 or 5 percent, which would effectively allow the dong to depreciate.\par \par \uc2 "This is more or less good news," a trader at a major Vietnamese bank said. "It will make it a little bit easier for the market to move, but it is not going to make things any better in the long term. ... I think the market is going to hit the ceiling soon."\par \par \uc2 Vietnam has had a rough year. It battled double-digit inflation and a widening trade deficit in the first two quarters by tightening monetary policy, but with inflation starting to ease authorities have pulled out the stops to offset the dampening effect of the global credit crisis on growth.\par \par \uc2 The \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  last month for the third time this year widened the daily band in which the dong is allowed to move, increasing it to 3 percent either side of a fixed mid-point set each day, from 2 percent.\par \par \uc2 Vietnam estimated its trade deficit this year would grow 20.6 percent to $17 billion, below earlier government forecasts, as exports jumped 29.5 percent, the highest growth since 2004, a state-run newspaper reported on Wednesday.\par \par \uc2 Economists at a coffee conference this month said that the dong could be devalued next year due to a worsening balance of payments and bad bank debts while the government also needed to prop up exports hit by the global economic downturn.\par \par \uc2 The dollar/dong exchange rate should have been lowered to 17,000 dong or 18,000 dong per dollar, an economist told Reuters at the conference. (Additional reporting by John Ruwitch in Hanoi and Daniel Bases in New York; Editing by Jonathan Oatis)\par \par \uc2 VIETNAM-ECONOMY/\b \highlight22\uc2 CURRENCY\b0 \highlight\uc2  (UPDATE 2)|LANGEN|ABN|D|M|E|RBN|RNP   \par \par \uc2 Dokument LBA0000020081224e4co000oy\par }\page {\par\fs20{\*\shppict{\pict\pngblip\picw172\pich40\picscaley100\picscalex100\picwgoal2580\pichgoal600
