\par\par \b \uc2 Emerging  \highlight22\uc2 FX \highlight\uc2 -Asia down on Fed outlook, Vietnam cuts dong\b0\par\par\uc2 By Kevin Yao   \par \uc2 855 \uc2 W\u246\'00\'F6rter\par \uc2 11 Juni 2008\par 05:18\par \uc2 Reuters News\par \uc2 LBA\par \uc2 Englisch\par \uc2 (c) 2008 Reuters Limited   \par \par \uc2 SINGAPORE, June 11 (Reuters) - Asian \b \highlight22\uc2 currencies\b0 \highlight\uc2  fell on Wednesday after remarks by U.S. officials fanned expectations of higher U.S. interest rates this year, while Vietnam cut the official dong rate in what analysts described as an effective devaluation.\par \par \uc2 The Thai baht <THB=TH> fell as far as 33.18 per dollar, down nearly 0.7 percent from late Asian trade on Tuesday. Suspected \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2  to support the \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  helped keep it above a five-month low of 33.41 hit on Monday.\par \par \uc2 Traders attributed th baht's weakness to the dollar's broad strength and expected the baht to move in tight ranges as the Bank of Thailand has been \b \highlight22\uc2 intervening\b0 \highlight\uc2  in \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  markets to support the baht.\par \par \uc2 "They (the \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2 ) have been in the market since 33.1-33.13 this morning," said a Bangkok-based trader.\par \par \uc2 Central banks in South Korea, the Philippines, India, and Indonesia have also sold dollars in recent sessions to limit the declines in their \b \highlight22\uc2 currencies\b0 \highlight\uc2  out of concern that further weakness could stoke inflationary risks.\par \par \uc2 "We view regional \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  action as consistent with the anti-inflation bias of monetary policy and expect continued policy action to stabilise the markets," \uc2 JPMorgan\uc2  strategists Claudio Piron and Yen Ping Ho said in a note.\par \par \uc2 The dollar hit a 3-1/2-month high versus the yen <JPY=> and held gains against the euro <EUR=> after more U.S. officials talked tougher on inflation, cementing expectations that the Federal Reserve would raise interest rates later this year.\par \par \uc2 Dallas Fed President Richard Fisher said on Tuesday the U.S. \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  would not allow inflation expectations to rise unchecked, echoing comments made by Fed Chairman Ben Bernanke a day earlier. [ID:nN10365737]\par \par \uc2 The warnings about inflation came amid a barrage of remarks from U.S. officials saying they are keeping an eye on the U.S. \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  while leaving options open for dollar-buying \b \highlight22\uc2 intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2  to stem its slide. [ID:nN10365737]\par \par \b \highlight22\uc2 VIETNAM\b0 \highlight\uc2  CUTS DONG RATE\par \par \uc2 Vietnam's \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  cut the dong by almost 2 percent against the dollar on Wednesday to a record low in what analysts described as an effective devaluation of the \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  after inflation in the country surged to 25 percent in May.\par \par \uc2 Some analysts have warned about a possible \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  crisis in Vietnam as the offshore non-deliverable forwards now imply a nearly 30 percent plunge in the dong's value in the next year.\par \par \uc2 "Vietnam is the closest to a crisis but the conditions that brought it there are present elsewhere," said Tim Condon, head of Asia research at ING.\par \par \uc2 "We consider Indonesia and the Philippines the most vulnerable to contagion from Vietnam," he said in a note.\par \par \uc2 Rising inflation has taken a toll on the South Korean won <KRW=>, the Philippines peso <PHP=> and Indian rupee <INR=IN>. These three \b \highlight22\uc2 currencies\b0 \highlight\uc2  have fallen against the dollar so far this year, while other Asian \b \highlight22\uc2 currencies\b0 \highlight\uc2  are firmer.\par \par \uc2 The peso fell as much as a fifth of a percent on Wednesday to 44.50 per dollar, its weakest level since October.\par \par \uc2 The won lost nearly 0.4 percent to 1,030 per dollar, but the rupee edged up to 42.857 per dollar amid expectations that foreign investors may resume buying of local shares.\par \par \uc2 Elsewhere, the Singapore dollar <SGD=> fell to 1.3740 per U.S. dollar at one point, down half of a percent.\par \par \uc2 The Chinese yuan <CNY=CFXS> retreated as far as 6.9252 per dollar from its post-revaluation high of 6.9140 hit on Tuesday.\par \par \uc2 But investors still expect the Singapore dollar and the yuan to gain further this year as the authorities favour using stronger exchange rates as a policy lever to fight inflation. \b \highlight22\uc2 CURRENCIES\b0 \highlight\uc2  VS U.S. DOLLAR Change on the day at 0735 GMT \par \par \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 Currency\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2     Latest bid   Previous day    Pct Move\par  Japan yen       107.42         106.67       -0.70\par  Sing dlr        1.3702         1.3674       -0.20\par  Taiwan dlr      30.373         30.360       -0.04\par  Korean won     1029.75        1026.30       -0.34\par  Baht             33.15          32.96       -0.56\par  Peso             44.30          44.43       +0.28\par  Rupiah         9307.00        9327.00       +0.21\par  Rupee            42.87          42.96       +0.22\par  Ringgit         3.2670         3.2615       -0.17\par  Yuan            6.9208         6.9255       +0.07\par  Change so far in 2008\par  \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 Currency\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2     Latest bid  End prev year    Pct Move\par  Japan yen       107.42         111.33       +3.64\par  Sing dlr        1.3702         1.4382       +4.96\par  Taiwan dlr      30.373         32.443       +6.82\par  Korean won     1029.75         935.70       -9.13\par  Baht             33.15          33.68       +1.61\par  Peso       