\par\par \b \uc2 UPDATE 1-Vietnam says no plans for  \highlight22\uc2 currency \highlight\uc2  devaluation\b0\par\par\uc2 606 \uc2 W\u246\'00\'F6rter\par \uc2 6 Juni 2008\par 10:36\par \uc2 Reuters News\par \uc2 LBA\par \uc2 Englisch\par \uc2 (c) 2008 Reuters Limited   \par \par \uc2  (Adds details, market rates, comments)\par \par \uc2  HANOI, June 6 (Reuters) - Vietnam's government has no plans to devalue the dong in spite of the forwards markets pricing in a sharp depreciation in the value of the \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  one year from now, the country's second-largest bank BIDV said on Friday.\par \par \uc2  The government also has sufficient foreign \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  reserves to meet investors' demands to convert their funds into dollars, Prime Minister Nguyen Tan Dung was quoted as saying in the bank's statement.\par \par \uc2  Vietnam will strive to reduce its inflation to single digits by 2010, Dung told BIDV Chairman Tran Bac Ha and \uc2 JPMorgan\uc2 's Head of Economic and Sovereign Research, David Fernandez, at a meeting on Thursday, BIDV said.\par \par \uc2  Vietnam's economy and the dong are under pressure from inflation running at more than 25 percent and rising imports.\par \par \uc2  The country's overall balance of payments for the first five months of the year was a surplus $1 billion, Dung said.\par \par \uc2  "Given the current \b \highlight22\uc2 forex\b0 \highlight\uc2  surplus, the prime minister believed that the dollar/dong trading band should be adjusted in a flexible manner in both directions," the statement quoted Dung as saying of the current daily band of +/-1 percent.\par \par \uc2  In the short term, the band could be changed to come close to the +/- 2 percent as projected by the government, he said.\par \par \uc2  Analysts said the remarks would help calm market jitters over recent volatility.\par \par \uc2  "I think the market has seen some dramatic moves (in Vietnam) recently, and the comments are aimed at trying to calm down the market's nerves over how policy makers are dealing with the inflation problem," said David Mann, \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  strategist at Standard Chartered Bank in Hong Kong.\par \par \uc2  "We are expecting more of a smooth weakening of the dong than the NDF market suggests -- some market expectations do appear to be overdone."\par \par \uc2  VOLATILITY\par \par \uc2  Earlier on Friday, BIDV called on the \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  to take more action to stabilise the dong as it fell to fresh lows under pressure from rising import prices [ID:nHAN269583].\par \par \uc2  In offshore non-deliverable forwards <PNDG>, the dong firmed to 22,800/23,800 per dollar on one-year term by 0848 GMT, implying the \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  will be worth around 30 percent less in a year's time.\par \par \uc2  The dong's <VND=> spot rate was at 16,283/16,285 per dollar.\par \par \uc2  Dung said offshore markets which priced in a fall of between 20 and 40 percent in forwards deals had no grounds doing so.\par \par \uc2  He said the \uc2 State Bank of \b \highlight22\uc2 Vietnam\b0 \highlight\uc2  would step up its measures to \b \highlight22\uc2 intervene\b0 \highlight\uc2  in the markets.\par \par \uc2  "The government will soon publish its \b \highlight22\uc2 foreign exchange\b0 \highlight\uc2  reserves in dollars," the BIDV statement said, adding that Dung has asked the \uc2 State Bank of Vietnam\uc2  to publish the reserves on a regular basis to win the public's trust.\par \par \uc2  Dung assured the two bankers at the meeting that Vietnam would not conduct any policy to control foreign portfolio investments, which would contravene the country's commitments toward integration and the market economy it pursues.\par \par \uc2  Mann of Standard Chartered said rising oil prices have had a major impact on the economy and inflation was likely to climb.\par \par \uc2  "We are still expecting inflation, which is quite high right now, to go even higher in the third quarter," Mann said. "But we think it will start coming off by the fourth quarter and the first quarter of next year."\par \par \uc2  (Reporting by Ho Binh Minh and Kevin Yao, editing by Jacqueline Wong)  \par \par \uc2 VIETNAM-ECONOMY/ (UPDATE 1)|LANGEN|ABN|M|E|RBN|D|SI|RNP|DNP   \par \par \uc2 Dokument LBA0000020080606e466000pe\par }\page {\par\fs20{\*\shppict{\pict\pngblip\picw172\pich40\picscaley100\picscalex100\picwgoal2580\pichgoal600
