\par\par \b \uc2  Vietnam Money- \highlight22\uc2 Central bank \highlight\uc2  seen stabilising rates. \b0\par\par\uc2 578 \uc2 W\u246\'00\'F6rter\par \uc2 24 M\u228\'00\'E4rz 2003\par 10:03\par \uc2 Reuters News\par \uc2 LBA\par \uc2 Englisch\par \uc2 (c) 2003 Reuters Limited\par \par \uc2  HANOI, March 24 (Reuters) - Facing a weakened dollar on growing concerns that the U.S.-led war in Iraq could take longer than some had expected, \b \highlight22\uc2 Vietnam\b0 \highlight\uc2 's \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  is expected to \b \highlight22\uc2 intervene\b0 \highlight\uc2  to keep exchange rates stable to boost exports. \par \par \uc2  "The \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  will have to step in if things get out of control," said a banker at a local bank in Hanoi on Monday. \par \par \b \highlight22\uc2 Central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  deputy governor Phung Khac Ke was quoted on Monday by Dau Tu (Investment) newspaper as saying that the bank would be "flexible in regulating \b \highlight22\uc2 foreign exchange operations\b0 \highlight\uc2  to secure support for exports" in case the war in Iraq drags on. \par \par \uc2  Last month, Vietnam raised its export growth for 2003 to $18.5 billion from $18 billion set late last year by the National Assembly, the country's highest legislative body, after exports in 2002 rose 10 percent year-on-year to $16.53 billion. \par \par \uc2  The dollar fell against most major \b \highlight22\uc2 currencies\b0 \highlight\uc2  on Monday including the euro and the yen on fear a prolonged war would pressure an already expanding fiscal deficit and undermine the greenback. \par \par \uc2  Apart from the possible negative impact of a weakened dollar, the U.S.-led strike on Iraq is taking a toll on local exporters. \par \par \uc2  Vietnam shipped $500 million of annual agricultural exports to Iraq last year. Shipments were cancelled or put on hold after the United States launched its attack on Iraq last Thursday. \par \par \uc2  "Demand for the dong has already firmed up as businesses move into implementation and disbursement stage of their projects," said Pham Van Thang, director of Habubank branch in Hanoi whose main clients are from the private sector. \par \par \uc2  DONG DEMAND STRONG \par \par \uc2  "So far companies are still on an expansionary track so the demands for the dong will be strong," Thang added. \par \par \uc2  Besides the private sector's growing needs for loans in the local \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2 , the demand for the dong is principally for funding major projects including the building of the north-south highway, a $1.3 billion oil refinery and several power plants, bankers noted. \par \par \uc2  "Strong domestic demand for the dong will help offset the impact of a weakening of the dollar on the dollar-dong exchange rates," said a financial expert in Hanoi. \par \par \uc2  On Monday, the \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  set the dong at 15,414 against the dollar, eight points higher from a week ago. \par \par \uc2  Despite signs of a weakening dollar, local bankers said they are sticking to the forecast that the dong would devalue by three percent against the dollar this year, compared with 2.1 percent in 2002. \par \par \uc2  Meanwhile, rates of short-term dong loans on the tiny interbank market have gone back down to the level two weeks ago, with overnight loans this week at 4.50-5.00 percent, against 4.50-5.25 percent last week. \par \par \uc2  Banks also raised interest rates on gold deposits following a recent fall in world gold prices. State media said the Bank for Exports and Imports (Eximbank) has added a 0.3 percentage point to its annual interest rates on gold deposits for all terms. \par \par \uc2  Its rate on the 12-month deposits now stands at 3.90 percent, said the Vietnam Economic Times newspaper. \par \par \uc2  Vietnam produces less than two tonnes of gold per year and imports between 35 and 40 tonnes for local consumption. Domestic gold prices eased to $315.49 an ounce on Monday from $323.88 last Wednesday. ($1=15,414 dong). \par \par \uc2 Dokument lba0000020030324dz3o00i84\par }\page {\par\fs20{\*\shppict{\pict\pngblip\picw184\pich22\picscaley100\picscalex100\picwgoal2760\pichgoal330
